    "content": "## Using the Transaction Checklist\n\nChecklists keep earnest money, HOA, objections, and lender milestones from slipping through crack-of-busy. Treat them as living—not ceremonial.\n\n### Auto-complete behavior\n\nSome items complete themselves when the document story catches up—examples your admin might enable:\n\n1. A recorded **approved** status on a linked document when DocuSign completes.\n2. Title milestones when a commitment uploads—or similar integrations your brokerage wires.\n\nIf something auto-completes unexpectedly, **verify the underlying artifact** before calling it done.\n\n> **Gold rule:** Automation saves clicks—not accountability. Eyes still belong on material facts.\n\n### Manual completion\n\nWhen you’ve verified something without a machine cue:\n\n1. Toggle the item once you’ve confirmed against source—PDF, email confirmation, or MLS evidence as policy requires.\n2. If you toggle in error, flip it back—your audit trail matters less than correctness.\n\n### Due dates\n\nWhere due dates exist:\n\n1. Use realistic dates tied to contract timelines—not every item needs tomorrow’s urgency.\n2. Teach assistants that moving dates without narrative causes chaos—note why when you shift.\n\n### PDF export\n\nIf your workspace exports checklist summaries:\n\n1. Export after a milestone—preliminary HUD review, lender conditions cleared, final walk photos uploaded—whatever your office uses for file QA.\n2. Name exports with **property + milestone** so recipients open the right packet.\n\n> **Remember:** Checklists mirror **risk**, not busywork—what’s on the list should map to something your broker-in-charge would defend.\n"
