    "content": "## Inviting Parties to the Transaction\n\nInvites set expectations before someone asks “why can’t I see that?” in all caps. Send them early enough to be useful—late enough that facts aren’t fiction.\n\n### How to invite each role\n\n1. From the transaction file, open **Parties** (or your tenant’s equivalent).\n2. Choose **Invite** beside the role—**buyer**, **seller**, **listing agent**, **buyer agent**, **lender**, **title**, or **coordinator** slots your template exposes.\n3. Enter the **work email** your policy requires—personal Gmail invites often break brokerage compliance rules.\n4. Send. The recipient accepts, sets a password (or SSO if configured), and lands in a **scoped** workspace.\n\n> **Tip:** Invite coordinators and agents before consumers when paperwork is still volatile—fewer confused homeowners.\n\n### What each party can see\n\nBy design, people see **their transaction slice**, not your whole pipeline:\n\n- **Buyers / sellers** — Milestones, documents shared with them, messages on their file—**not** other clients’ deals.\n- **Agents** — Collaboration views tied to representation—typically broader than consumers, narrower than internal TC admin.\n- **Mortgage / title** — Conditions and docs relevant to their lane—**not** unrelated brokerage chatter unless you explicitly share.\n\n> **Important:** Row-level security is your safety rail—don’t screenshot confidential packets into email when NEXA already scoped access.\n\n### Resending invites\n\n1. Find the party row—status usually shows **pending**.\n2. Choose **Resend invite**—don’t create duplicate party rows with the same email; it fractures audit trails.\n\n### Revoking access\n\nWhen someone leaves the deal—or you invited the wrong address:\n\n1. Use **Remove access** or your tenant’s revoke control on that party row.\n2. Confirm downstream—signatures in flight may still complete depending on provider settings.\n\n> **Warm clarity:** If you revoke a buyer’s spouse accidentally, apologize fast and re-invite—stress shows up as mistakes at COE.\n"
